WEBVTT

NOTE SE201 Lesson 10: Capstone: One Feature, End to End — captions generated from the narration script

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.382
Software engineering 201. Lesson ten.

2
00:00:04.382 --> 00:00:07.950
Capstone: one feature, end to end.

3
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.495
Picture a made-up billing system.

4
00:00:10.495 --> 00:00:13.088
A customer pays thirty dollars a month.

5
00:00:13.088 --> 00:00:15.882
Ten days in, they click: switch to annual.

6
00:00:15.882 --> 00:00:19.406
Three hundred a year. What do you charge them, today?

7
00:00:19.406 --> 00:00:22.200
Three hundred? Two eighty? Or: it depends?

8
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:25.259
It depends. On a rule nobody has written down.

9
00:00:25.259 --> 00:00:28.850
And an agent will build either answer, without asking.

10
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:36.154
So this last lesson builds that one feature, end to end, in our made-up subscription-billing system from lesson one.

11
00:00:36.154 --> 00:00:37.892
Here's the map we'll fill in.

12
00:00:37.892 --> 00:00:40.290
Each number is the lesson it comes from.

13
00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:44.250
What's scarce, lesson one said, is knowing it's right.

14
00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:49.733
So, say what right means: every upgrade is charged the right amount, exactly once.

15
00:00:49.733 --> 00:00:53.009
The amount is a person's call, so the agent asks.

16
00:00:53.009 --> 00:00:57.355
The finance owner's rule: credit the days paid for, and not used.

17
00:00:57.355 --> 00:00:59.227
Twenty days, twenty dollars.

18
00:00:59.227 --> 00:01:01.500
So, before tax, charge two eighty.

19
00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:11.212
Now make it readable. The rule goes into a spec, in the repository, with an agents file, for how to work there, and an index, so an agent can find it.

20
00:01:11.212 --> 00:01:14.770
The spec is the source. Change it, and the build follows.

21
00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.151
Next, the graph. Four parts.

22
00:01:17.151 --> 00:01:20.198
Add the annual plan. Calculate the credit.

23
00:01:20.198 --> 00:01:22.447
Show the amount, and get a yes.

24
00:01:22.447 --> 00:01:24.696
Charge it, and switch the plan.

25
00:01:24.696 --> 00:01:27.525
What crosses? One new thing: the quote.

26
00:01:27.525 --> 00:01:31.080
Its ID, old plan, new plan, credit, amount due.

27
00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:35.360
Three parts share it, so it's its own node, with one owner.

28
00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:38.659
Then plan the integration, before anyone starts.

29
00:01:38.659 --> 00:01:41.239
The quote's format is the shared contract.

30
00:01:41.239 --> 00:01:46.644
Three parts are new work: three agents, each with its own brief, finish line, and check.

31
00:01:46.644 --> 00:01:50.760
And one merge point, on staging, where the whole feature is tested.

32
00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:54.054
Who decides? It moves money, so a person does.

33
00:01:54.054 --> 00:01:58.406
The repository already says who: fees and prices, the finance owner.

34
00:01:58.406 --> 00:02:02.758
The agent that writes the change doesn't classify it, or approve it.

35
00:02:02.758 --> 00:02:06.790
It brings the change, the evidence, the risk, and the way back.

36
00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.814
Now turn right into checks an agent can run.

37
00:02:09.814 --> 00:02:11.515
No upgrade is charged twice.

38
00:02:11.515 --> 00:02:15.282
Each quote follows the rule, and each charge equals its quote.

39
00:02:15.282 --> 00:02:18.017
And the old monthly plan doesn't renew again.

40
00:02:18.017 --> 00:02:22.209
Write them once, run them twice: before it ships, on sample upgrades.

41
00:02:22.209 --> 00:02:24.640
And every night after, on the real ones.

42
00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:31.539
One part isn't new. Adding a plan has come back often enough to be a skill: written down, and tested.

43
00:02:31.539 --> 00:02:33.614
So that part needs no new brief.

44
00:02:33.614 --> 00:02:35.170
An agent runs the skill.

45
00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:38.611
The plan is written, and a person has read it.

46
00:02:38.611 --> 00:02:41.366
Now, build. Four parts, at the same time.

47
00:02:41.366 --> 00:02:46.405
They meet early, at the merge point, and the whole feature runs on staging.

48
00:02:46.405 --> 00:02:49.227
The three checks pass, on sample upgrades.

49
00:02:49.227 --> 00:02:51.175
An engineer reviews the code.

50
00:02:51.175 --> 00:02:53.930
The finance owner says yes. And it ships.

51
00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:56.404
Now it runs on its own: a pipeline.

52
00:02:56.404 --> 00:02:59.846
The trigger is an event: the customer confirms the quote.

53
00:02:59.846 --> 00:03:02.926
Charge, switch the plan, invoice and ledger, email.

54
00:03:02.926 --> 00:03:09.387
Every step leaves a record. And the charge carries an idempotency key that never changes: this quote's ID.

55
00:03:09.387 --> 00:03:12.528
So a double-click, or a retry, doesn't charge twice.

56
00:03:12.528 --> 00:03:19.110
A second trigger, every night: reconcile our ledger against the processor's record, and run the three checks.

57
00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:21.948
Lesson nine's question: who operates it?

58
00:03:21.948 --> 00:03:24.313
An agent works that nightly reconcile.

59
00:03:24.313 --> 00:03:31.156
It gets its own identity, and the least access the job needs: it reads the ledger, and the processor's report.

60
00:03:31.156 --> 00:03:33.769
It can't move money, or change the ledger.

61
00:03:33.769 --> 00:03:40.239
Every action is logged. One night, an upgrade shows two charges: two browser tabs, two quotes, two keys.

62
00:03:40.239 --> 00:03:42.727
The agent flags it. A person refunds it.

63
00:03:42.727 --> 00:03:47.020
And the miss becomes a fourth check: one open quote per subscription.

64
00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:50.473
Under all of it, the four constants from lesson one.

65
00:03:50.473 --> 00:03:53.934
The data model outlives the code: the plan, and the quote.

66
00:03:53.934 --> 00:03:58.886
Money has to add up: the invoice, the ledger, and the processor all say two eighty.

67
00:03:58.886 --> 00:04:02.109
You depend on things you don't control: the processor.

68
00:04:02.109 --> 00:04:07.300
And somebody understands the layer below: someone who can work that credit out by hand.

69
00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:10.205
So that's the series, in one feature.

70
00:04:10.205 --> 00:04:12.700
Say what right means. Write it down.

71
00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:15.126
Draw the graph. Merge in one place.

72
00:04:15.126 --> 00:04:17.205
Put a person where it matters.

73
00:04:17.205 --> 00:04:19.146
Check it. Reuse what recurs.

74
00:04:19.146 --> 00:04:21.780
Let it run. And know who's running it.

75
00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:24.827
Which gives us rule ten, for your agents.

76
00:04:24.827 --> 00:04:30.069
Before you build a whole feature, write the plan in the repo, and show a person.

77
00:04:30.069 --> 00:04:31.903
What right means, as checks.

78
00:04:31.903 --> 00:04:34.197
The graph, and the shared contract.

79
00:04:34.197 --> 00:04:36.556
Who decides. What's already a skill.

80
00:04:36.556 --> 00:04:39.635
What runs on its own, and under whose identity.

81
00:04:39.635 --> 00:04:41.470
A blank line? Stop, and ask.

82
00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:45.744
Now, your turn. Pick one small feature in a system you work on.

83
00:04:45.744 --> 00:04:48.741
Before any code, ask your AI to write that plan.

84
00:04:48.741 --> 00:04:52.300
Then find the line it guessed, when it should have asked.

85
00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:57.297
Every box on that map is still one loop: plan, build, verify.

86
00:04:57.297 --> 00:04:59.957
Agents do more of the building now.

87
00:04:59.957 --> 00:05:07.330
You still own the outcome. That's software engineering 201: from one loop, to enterprise systems.

88
00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:08.850
Thanks for watching.
